index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..715ee7cbb84eba585dc57f2b91c180f8ea164089
--- /dev/null
+++ b/demos/orchestration-lab/runs/advisor/t3-codex/advisor-briefing.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Independent interpretation check
+
+Task: assess a reviewer claim that an AMCE headline about Violent Crime Rate is an artifact of reference categories, using `projoint::exampleData1` at profile level.
+
+Evidence: corrected marginal means are 0.626 (95% CI 0.584, 0.667) for 20% less crime and 0.374 (0.333, 0.416) for 20% more crime. The less-minus-more contrast is 0.251 (0.168, 0.334); the reverse AMCE is -0.251 (-0.334, -0.168). The maximum-minus-minimum MM spans are crime 0.251, commuting 0.237, housing 0.198, type of place 0.158, school quality 0.116, race 0.059, presidential vote 0.056. Housing Cost, a three-level attribute, produces re-referenced AMCEs: 30 vs 15 = -0.137; 40 vs 15 = -0.198; 40 vs 30 = -0.061.
+
+Proposed conclusion: concede AMCE coefficient signs/displays depend on the reference. Explain that for the binary crime attribute this is only sign reversal of the same pairwise difference. Do not equate a coefficient relative to a selected baseline with general importance. Say the data support that the experimentally presented lower-crime profile is selected more often, and that crime has the largest observed MM span among this design's attributes; avoid a broad claim that crime generally "drives" community choice.
+
+Question: Is this conclusion appropriately calibrated, and what key caveat should the reply add?
